{
 "id": "rozhinkes-mit-mandlen",
 "version": 1,
 "reviewed": false,
 "title": "Rozhinkes mit Mandlen",
 "description": "Abraham Goldfaden's Yiddish lullaby from the opera Shulamith, as S. Schenker's New York sheet music printed it, for 2 to 4 singers, sung in Yiddish (the words are shown in Latin letters): the lower voices hum softly under the verse and join in the words of the refrain. In a corner of the Temple the widowed Daughter of Zion rocks her only son to sleep, singing that a little white goat stands under his cradle and that raisins and almonds will be his trade.",
 "language": "yi",
 "difficulty": "intermediate",
 "genre": [
  "yiddish",
  "lullaby",
  "folk"
 ],
 "style": "free",
 "tempo": 57,
 "key": "Em",
 "timeSignature": "6/8",
 "pickupBeats": 0.333333,
 "credits": {
  "words": "Abraham Goldfaden",
  "tune": "Abraham Goldfaden",
  "arrangement": "Stackapella"
 },
 "licence": {
  "words": "public domain: Abraham Goldfaden died 1908",
  "tune": "public domain: Abraham Goldfaden died 1908",
  "arrangement": "original, written for Stackapella under the tune as A. R. Zagler's edition prints it; his piano part was used only to check the chords; the lower voices hum the verse and the refrain's quick bar \"dos tsigele iz geforn\", and sing the rest of the refrain's words, leaving the soprano alone on the quick pickups \"Unter\" and \"shteyt a\"",
  "recordings": "synthesized guides",
  "checked": null,
  "source": "tune and words from \"Raisins and Almonds. (Solo Absalom.)\", No. 6, \"Arr. by A. R. Zagler\", voice and piano (the refrain printed with the voice in the piano's right hand), Andante, in E minor in 6/8, footed \"Shulamith. 50.\"; its cover reads \"ראזשינקעס מיט מאַנדלען\", \"From The Opera 'Shulamith'\", with a portrait captioned \"A. Goldfaden\", \"Published by S. Schenker, 66 Canal Street, New York\"; undated (the Brown Digital Repository, bdr:100085, gives 1917 and credits Abraham Goldfaden, 1840-1908; IMSLP gives about 1910); read from the scans at repository.library.brown.edu/studio/item/bdr:100085 (pages 1 to 3), kept here in E minor at 57 to the dotted quarter; the tune checked against Simon Adler's piano fantasia \"Sulamitha. 'Raisines & Almonds.'\", Op. 46, in D minor (bdr:100759, dated 1890 by Brown). Goldfaden died 1908; Zagler's dates are unknown, so only Goldfaden's tune and words are taken, and the four parts are our own, his piano used only to check the chords. The tune is the print's vocal line except: its grace notes on \"shlo-\" and \"li-\" are left out; the solo's \"Ah\" flourish before the refrain (six sixteenths and a held E with a fermata) is left out, the soprano resting there while the lower voices hold their chord; \"klor\" takes the print's first two B's as one note; \"leyn\" is cut to a sixteenth for a breath before \"ir ben\"; both held \"le\" syllables, \"ruf,\" and the refrain's two held \"dlen\" syllables each give up half a beat for a breath; and the last \"shlof\" is held to the end of the bar instead of the print's piano postlude. The Yiddish is romanized from the print's German-style spelling in YIVO style, in standard YIVO spelling: j as y, ch as kh, w as v, z as ts, tz as ts, sch and sh as sh, s before a vowel and final s in is as z (sitzt, sie, singt, sein, is as zitst, zi, zingt, zayn, iz), she as zhe, ie and ih as i (die, wiegt, ihr, ihm as di, vigt, ir, im), ei as ey or ay as the word has it (beis, chei-der, a-lein, weis, sein, dein as beys, khey-der, a-leyn, vays, zayn, dayn), eh as ey (shteht, shehn as shteyt, sheyn), oh as o (klohr as klor), und as un, ke-se-der as ke-sey-der; the verse's \"Ju-de-len\" is written Yi-de-len as the refrain spells it (ji-de-les), and \"ro-sin-kes\" ro-zhin-kes as the cover's Yiddish title spells it; \"mik-dosh\" and \"Zi-jon\" (Tsi-yon) keep the print's sung vowels, where standard YIVO spelling would give beys-hamikdesh and bas-tsiyen; the ending after l is written -len as YIVO spells it (han-dlen, man-dlen); syllables are written as the print sings them (vin-kl, yo-khi-dl, shlo-fn, ge-fo-rn)"
 },
